---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="PARC Website" description="Redesigned website for the Pediatric Anxiety Research Center at Brown.">
  <a href="/making" class="eyebrow project-back">← making</a>
  <h1>Pediatric Anxiety Research Center</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Fall 2025 team project with Full Stack at Brown — our group was assigned to redesign the public
    site for the Pediatric Anxiety Research Center, a clinical research program at Bradley Hospital.
  </p>

  <div class="project-actions">
    <a class="cta" href="https://www.parcanxiety.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Visit live site →</a>
  </div>

  <p class="project-meta">Frontend Developer · Providence, RI · Fall 2025</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The Org</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.fullstackatbrown.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Full Stack at Brown</a>
      is Brown's student web development organization. Since 2019, FSAB has built websites on a
      pro-bono basis for local nonprofits, student groups, and research teams. PARC was our semester
      assignment — a real client, a real deadline, and a team split across design, frontend, and backend.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Client</h2>
    <p>
      PARC has provided assessment and treatment for children with OCD and anxiety disorders at
      Bradley Hospital since 1999. Their old site buried important paths — outpatient services,
      intensive programs, active research studies — under layers of clinical language. Families
      looking for help shouldn't have to hunt for it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="project-grid">
    <div>
      <h2>My Role</h2>
      <p>
        I joined as frontend developer on a small team. My work sat at the intersection of design
        handoff and backend integration: turning layouts into responsive pages, building new sections
        for grant and research content, and iterating through code reviews and design discussions
        with the rest of the group.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What We Shipped</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A responsive redesign with clearer navigation across treatment, research, and training
          pages.
        </li>
        <li>
          New feature areas for community mental health grant initiatives and research study
          information — built in close collaboration with our designer and backend engineers.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A Note</h2>
    <p>
      Working on a site whose audience includes anxious parents made navigation clarity feel less
      like polish and more like care. Small UX decisions — where a phone number lives, how a
      program page opens — mattered more here than on most projects I've touched.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .project-back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .project-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.6rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .project-meta {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  section {
    margin: 2.2rem 0;
  }

  .project-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.2fr) minmax(220px, 0.8fr);
    gap: 2rem;
  }

  li {
    margin: 0.45rem 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .project-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
